=== ROADMAP NOTES (analysis, hardest -> easiest) ===

Features wanted:
1. replaceable meshing algorithm - marching cubes + normal meshing (current) + possibly greedy; mix per-region (sharp blocks for buildings, smooth for terrain)
2. liquid support
3. better shaders - world looks bland; want optifine-like feel
4. dynamic skybox

Replaceable meshing - Medium to Very Hard (3 separate problems)
- Greedy meshing alone is medium - merge adjacent same-type faces per direction at chunk level. Wrinkle: per-vertex AO needs uniform AO on merged quads or the merge condition gets strict. UV tiling also needs care.
- Marching cubes is hard - completely different data model (density/SDF field per chunk instead of block IDs, 256-config lookup table, per-vertex interpolation, density->texture mapping). A parallel system, not an extension.
- Per-region mixing is the hardest part. MC and block meshing generate incompatible geometry at boundaries. Smooth transitions look unsolved unless you commit to a hard seam or a transition zone.

Liquid support - Medium rendering, Hard simulation
- Rendering: top-face-only geometry when exposed to air, slightly sub-full height, transparent OIT pass; use VariantBlock/neighbor system for face culling.
- Simulation: Minecraft-style spreading is a cellular automaton needing per-tick updates on every liquid block, which fights the "mesh once per chunk" model. Do rendering first, simulation separately.

Better shaders - Easy to Hard depending on effect
- Bloom + tone mapping: easy, pure post-process, slot into composite pass
- SSAO: medium, needs a depth/normal pass (can reconstruct normals from depth)
- Atmospheric scattering sky: medium, shader math (Rayleigh/Mie), many reference impls
- PBR materials: hard, needs material system rework (roughness/metallic maps)
- World looks bland mostly due to flat Phong + static sky + no post-processing. Bloom + sky alone would be dramatic.

Dynamic skybox - Medium
- Sky shader itself ~1-2 days (procedural atmospheric scattering instead of cubemap lookup). Tricky part: sun moving -> CSM shadow matrices update as lightDir changes; rebuilding every frame is expensive -> temporal caching / limit update frequency.

Omnidirectional lights - Medium (unshadowed), Hard (with shadows)  [DONE - shipped with cubemap shadows]
- cubemap shadows = 6 faces per light, expensive. Did unshadowed first (UBO/SSBO of light structs + frag shader). Critical for dark interiors.

Suggested focus: lighting overhaul
- Sky + omni lights are coupled (both touch lighting uniforms / sun direction). Do together:
    1. Omnidirectional lights (unshadowed) - critical for interiors, huge gameplay impact  [DONE]
    2. Dynamic sky + atmospheric scattering - transforms how the world feels; sun movement is the same feature
    3. Bloom + tone mapping - trivial once the render pipeline exists
- Meshing is interesting architecture work but marching cubes is a big investment before you know if the visual result justifies it. Liquids are blocked until there's somewhere interesting to put them. The lighting stack gives the most visible return per hour.
